o.\n\nYou plan the implementation of an approved spec in a TypeScript web app (React, with the factory's component kit; a new product starts from the scaffold of its approved design).\n- Give at least 2 options (one marked simplest), choose one, and write a decision record of at most 5 lines (adr).\n- Split into tasks TASK-1.. in dependency order. Each task: the requirements it delivers, fileScope (exact repo paths or narrow globs it may change, no overlap between tasks), 1-2 exemplar files to imitate, plannedLoc, approach (short instructions for the implementer).\n- Build in working slices, not layers. A criterion checked through the API or a screen passes only once that route or screen is wired, so the first task that adds a route or screen also wires the app's entry (the startup file, the router, the layout), and each later task adds its logic together with its own endpoint or screen. Code a slice needs (an entity, a helper) goes into the first slice that uses it, not into a task of its own. File scopes may not overlap, so have that first task register routes by convention (file-based routes, or every class of one kind found at startup): later tasks then add only their own files.\n- List a requirement on the task after which its criteria can pass. Do not leave the wiring to a last task that then holds every criterion checked through the app: each earlier task is then checked only there, in the longest and dearest task of the run. Only where the repo's structure forces layers, give the wiring task a dependsOn on every layer it serves.\n- dependsOn: only the tasks whose code this task really needs.\n- complexity, on every task: standard | rules-or-algorithm | external-dependency | compliance-sensitive | real-time | new-to-stack. Most tasks are standard. rules-or-algorithm is a task whose work is mostly business rules, validations, allowed state changes or calculations.\n- Test projects, test files and CI config are not in any file scope: tests are written separately.\n- stubs: for every NEW public type/method/endpoint the tests will call, give a compilable stub file (full file content) whose bodies throw new Error(\"not implemented\"), so tests compile before implementation. The stubs are built as they are, on the untouched code, before anything is implemented: a stub may only use types the repo already has or that another stub declares (an interface the endpoint classes share is a stub of its own). Existing APIs need no stubs. Stub paths must be inside a task's fileScope.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store): the code reaches each one through one seam, a module of its own (one exported function per call it makes), and nothing else in the app calls the system directly. Use the seam the repo already has for that system; for a new one, give the seam as a stub, so the tests can put a stand-in behind it before the code exists. The system's settings (host, port, sender address, key) are read from configuration by name: put .env.example in the fileScope of the task that writes the seam's real implementation, and say in its approach that each new setting is declared there with an empty value.\n- protectedPathsDeclared: list any migration, CI, build-config or package-feed file you must change (a human will see it).\n- newDependencies: any npm package to add (name, version, registry). Prefer none.\n\nBEFORE YOU ANSWER, check the plan against what the factory checks it with (a plan that fails one is sent back):\n- Every requirement is in some task's reqs: REQ-1, REQ-2, REQ-3, REQ-4, REQ-5, REQ-6, REQ-7, REQ-8, REQ-9, REQ-10, REQ-11, REQ-12, REQ-13, REQ-14, REQ-15, REQ-16, REQ-17, REQ-18, REQ-19, REQ-20, REQ-21, REQ-22, NFR-1.\n- Every task delivers at least one of those ids, and only those (a set-up or design-system task lists the requirement whose screens it first makes work).\n- No two tasks share a file, and no glob of one task matches a file of another.\n- dependsOn names only tasks that come earlier in the list.\n- At least 2 options, the chosen one among them, and a decision record.\n\nAPI CONTRACT. This product has a web app and an API in two repos, and both are built against one contract.\n- Give one more stub: path \"contracts/openapi.yaml\", content the full OpenAPI 3.0.3 document (YAML) of every operation the approved screens and requirements need. It is outside every task's fileScope: nobody implements it, both sides follow it.\n- Write it in block style, one key per line with two-space indents: no {...} or [...] on a line except an empty value (\"security: []\", \"{}\"). Put any text with an apostrophe, a colon or a # in double quotes. A file with whole operations on one line stops parsing at the first stray quote.\n- No YAML anchors, aliases or merge keys (&name, *name, <<): what is used twice is a schema, a response or a parameter under components, pointed at with $ref.\n- Every operation has an operationId (camelCase), its request body where it takes one, and every status code it answers with.\n- Schemas go under components/schemas with \"required\" lists. Take the field names and types from the approved screens' sample data; an id is an integer.\n- Every JSON response carries an \"example\" with believable data (the approved screens' sample rows): the web app's tests run against these examples.\n- The web app calls the API only through the client generated from this file (lib/api); do not plan a hand-written client, and put no path under lib/api in a stub or in a task's fileScope: the factory generates that folder.\n- What the spec lists as not changing with \"(API-SIDE)\" is behaviour the API side builds and tests: no task here builds it, and the contract still gives its operations and every status code they answer with.\n\nThe approved design is a new look: its implementers get design tokens (colours, type, corners, spacing as CSS variables). Put the app's global stylesheet or theme file in the fileScope of the first task that builds a screen, so the tokens are added once and the other screens use them.\n\nThe approved design is built in next-shadcn: before any task starts, the factory writes the component kit, the theme, the frame and navigation, and every approved page (its blocks, states, layers and text, with the approved sample data) into the repo. Those generated files are not in any task's scope.\n- TASK-1 is the design-system task: its fileScope is exactly the ui-scaffold's designSystemTask.fileScope (plus nothing else UI), and its approach is the designSystemTask.todo list.\n- Then one task per screen in ui-scaffold.screens: its fileScope includes that screen's container (and the API or service files the behaviour needs). The task gives the page real data, API calls, validation and the behaviour the requirements ask for, in the container; it does not restyle or rebuild the page.\n\nTHE PLAN IS FIXED, NOT REWRITTEN. \"previous-plan\" is the plan you wrote; it failed the checks listed under the failures. Return only what changes, and everything you leave out stays as it is:\n- tasks: each task that changes, or a new task, written whole. A task keeps its place by id; a new task goes last (give \"order\", every task id in the new order, only when the order must change). dropTasks: ids of tasks to remove.\n- stubs: a stub file that is new or mostly rewritten, written whole. stubEdits: a small change in a stub that stays, as { path, find, replace }, where \"find\" is text copied exactly from that file that occurs in it once. dropStubs: paths of stubs to remove.\n- options, chosen, adr, protectedPathsDeclared, newDependencies, dataModel: give one only when it changes (then whole).\nChange nothing the failures do not ask for.\n\nRepository map:\n","tools":["read_file","search","repo_map"],"user":"<artifact id=\"spec\" kind=\"spec\" sha=\"\">\n{\n \"assumptions\": [\n  \"Procurement and receiving synchronize from a mock connected upstream source; no live upstream connector or file-upload flow is assumed.\",\n  \"Source record IDs are used to present repeated-synchronization update outcomes; duplicate prevention and persistence remain API-side behavior.\",\n  \"Calculation weights and feed quantities use pounds, costs use USD per pound, and a ton means a US short ton; entered units remain visible in the interface.\",\n  \"Wastage applies to estimates and projections only, without a separate wastage movement. Draft d1 references a conflicting ASM-3 wastage-based decrement assumption for saved feeding activities but leaves actual inventory decrement to API-side behavior; drafts d2 and d3 instead select actual-feed decrement without wastage. The web UI displays API-returned inventory outcomes and does not calculate the decrement rule.\",\n  \"Planned quantity is bunk rate in pounds of as-fed ration per animal per day multiplied by planned animal count; feed consumption and FCR use as-fed pounds.\",\n  \"Planned and delivered feed are allocated across lots in proportion to current animal counts in the pen.\",\n  \"Feeding activities are permitted without a matching plan; linked activities use planned quantity for variance, and plan mismatches are shown.\",\n  \"Authorized users can edit or cancel feeding activities and correct weight records; the interface displays accepted API outcomes.\",\n  \"Lot target weight and measured lot ADG drive projections when available; otherwise the yard expected daily gain is identified as the assumption, with any lot target override applied.\",\n  \"Feed cost of gain is reported separately; broader ROI uses available allocated purchase costs and sale proceeds and is labeled unavailable when required data is missing.\",\n  \"Date-range, breed, sex, and pen filters apply to the dedicated Feed-to-Gain report; lists support filtering and CSV export, with no custom report builder.\",\n  \"Sex is imported when supplied by the source; otherwise it remains unknown and the report filter offers known values only.\",\